T38.01|Financial data sources include issuer disclosures, exchanges, official statistics and commercial providers. Reliability depends on provenance, definitions, coverage and update timing. Licensing governs permitted storage, redistribution and use; access alone does not imply unlimited rights.~~Record the source and extraction date so another analyst can reproduce the work. A convenient dataset can still be unsuitable for the intended question.|Primary disclosure: Information from the issuer or authority.;;Market feed: Prices and activity under vendor conventions.;;Derived dataset: Transformed or estimated information.|Two datasets report revenue ₹100 and ₹110 lakh for the same company.~~Check consolidated versus standalone scope.;;Compare fiscal dates and units.;;Review whether one includes a later restatement.~~A discrepancy may reflect definitions rather than an error.~~Reuse permission requires the source's actual licence.|Record provenance and licence.;;Verify units and scope.;;Track update and restatement dates.
T38.02|Cleaning corrects or standardises data while reconciliation checks consistency with trusted totals and relationships. Duplicate keys, missing values, unit errors and mismatched periods can create convincing but false results. Cleaning decisions should be explicit and reversible.~~A join can multiply rows when keys are not unique. Validate grain before merging rather than fixing inflated totals afterward.|Validation: Checks allowed values and relationships.;;Reconciliation: Matches source totals or identities.;;Transformation: Applies documented cleaning rules.|A table has one revenue row ₹100 lakh; a joined lookup has two rows for the same company.~~The join creates two revenue rows.;;Summing now reports ₹200.;;Fix key uniqueness or use the correct relationship.~~Join structure can double a valid source value.~~The appropriate solution depends on the intended data grain.|Define grain and keys.;;Check duplicates and nulls.;;Reconcile totals before and after transformations.
T38.03|Corporate actions such as splits and dividends change price-series interpretation. A split changes share count and per-share price without creating wealth by itself. Total-return series include distributions under their reinvestment convention; raw prices and adjusted prices answer different questions.~~Adjustment methods and historical restatements differ by provider. Ensure dividends or splits are not counted twice.|Raw price: Observed market quotation.;;Split-adjusted price: Comparable share-unit basis.;;Total return: Includes specified distribution effects.|A share worth ₹100 undergoes a 2-for-1 split and trades at ₹50.~~One old share becomes two.;;Total holding value remains ₹100 before market movement.;;Raw price change alone would falsely imply a 50% loss.~~Units must be aligned before return calculation.~~Fees, dividend adjustments and taxes require separate treatment.|Read adjustment conventions.;;Reconcile share counts.;;Avoid double-counting distributions.
T38.04|Point-in-time data preserves what was available at each decision date, including publication and revision timing. Survivorship bias occurs when analysis excludes securities or entities that disappeared. Current databases often contain revised histories or only surviving names.~~Research must use the investable universe and information set that actually existed, rather than today's successful companies projected backward.|Publication timing: When information became available.;;Revision history: Later changes to earlier observations.;;Survivorship: Inclusion of failed or delisted entities.|A backtest uses year-end earnings released three months later to select shares at year-end.~~Move eligibility to the actual release date.;;Include companies later delisted.;;Recalculate performance with contemporaneous inputs.~~Future information and surviving winners can inflate apparent skill.~~Correct point-in-time coverage requires source evidence.|Store availability dates.;;Preserve revisions.;;Include failures and historical universe membership.
T38.05|Spreadsheet engineering builds models that are understandable, auditable and robust to input changes. Separate assumptions, calculations and outputs; use consistent units and explicit checks. Clear structure reduces errors without guaranteeing valid economic assumptions.~~Avoid hiding business logic inside unexplained hard-coded formulas. A model that balances can still omit capex, tax or funding needs.|Inputs: Documented assumptions and source data.;;Calculations: Transparent linked logic.;;Checks: Identities, signs and scenario consistency.|A model labels figures as ₹lakh but imports one debt balance of ₹1 crore as the number 1.~~Convert ₹1 crore to ₹100 lakh.;;Reconcile debt with source records.;;Add unit metadata and magnitude checks.~~Unit errors can overwhelm sophisticated formulas.~~Formatting alone cannot detect every error.|Separate inputs and formulas.;;Use consistent units and signs.;;Add reconciliation and scenario checks.
T38.06|SQL retrieves and combines structured records; Python and R support analysis, modelling and automation. Tool choice follows the task, data and reproducibility needs. Correct syntax does not ensure correct grain, economic interpretation or statistical assumptions.~~A reproducible workflow records dependencies, inputs and transformations. Review joins and filters before trusting a polished chart.|SQL: Queries structured tables.;;Python: General-purpose analysis and automation.;;R: Statistical computing and analysis ecosystem.|A returns table contains daily observations; an analyst averages them and labels the result annual return.~~Identify the daily frequency.;;Use an appropriate compounded period calculation.;;Check missing trading dates and distributions.~~Tool execution success does not validate the financial metric.~~Arithmetic mean and compounded return answer different questions.|Define the metric first.;;Validate grain and frequency.;;Record code, data and environment.
T38.07|APIs provide structured access to data or services; pipelines ingest, validate, transform and deliver it. Authentication, rate limits, errors and schema changes affect reliability. A response can be technically successful while containing stale, partial or revised data.~~Monitor freshness and completeness and retain lineage. Retries should avoid duplicate processing when operations are not idempotent.|Ingestion: Acquires source records.;;Validation: Tests schema, freshness and completeness.;;Delivery: Publishes controlled outputs.|A feed normally supplies 1,000 securities but today's response contains 600 with a successful status.~~Check expected coverage.;;Flag missing records instead of treating them as zero.;;Investigate provider status and refresh timing.~~Success status is not a completeness guarantee.~~Valid coverage expectations can change and need documentation.|Check timestamps and record counts.;;Handle errors and duplicates.;;Monitor schema and source changes.
T38.08|Econometric forecasting models relationships in data to estimate future outcomes. Explanation, prediction and causal inference are different goals. Correlation can aid prediction without proving that changing one variable will cause another to change.~~Time-series dependence, structural breaks and parameter uncertainty limit extrapolation. Compare forecasts with simple baselines using data unavailable during fitting.|Regression: Models conditional relationships.;;Time series: Uses temporal structure.;;Causal design: Seeks an identified intervention effect.|Sales and advertising both rise during a holiday period.~~A regression may find a positive association.;;Control for seasonality and consider reverse causality.;;Do not treat the coefficient automatically as incremental advertising return.~~Shared drivers can explain the association.~~Causal claims require a defensible identification strategy.|Define prediction versus causation.;;Check assumptions and dependence.;;Use out-of-sample baseline comparisons.
T38.09|Factor research tests whether defined characteristics relate to returns or risks. Backtesting simulates decisions using historical information and trading rules. Signal construction, universe, weighting, rebalance timing and cost assumptions determine the result.~~A successful backtest is evidence to examine, not proof of future returns. Economic rationale and independent validation help distinguish robust effects from fitted noise.|Signal: Characteristic used for selection or weighting.;;Portfolio rule: Converts signals into positions.;;Validation: Tests performance beyond the fitting sample.|A value strategy ranks 100 eligible shares by a published ratio and selects 20 monthly.~~Specify publication lags and missing-data rules.;;Calculate holdings before observing next-period returns.;;Include turnover, costs and delisted securities.~~The strategy must be executable with the historical information set.~~Results depend on constraints and implementation.|State exact rules.;;Use point-in-time inputs.;;Test costs and independent samples.
T38.10|Look-ahead bias uses future information; overfitting tunes a model too closely to noise; data snooping repeatedly searches until an attractive result appears. Each can produce apparent performance without a durable economic edge.~~Keep a genuinely untouched evaluation set and account for the number of trials. Repeatedly checking the test set makes it another training set.|Look-ahead: Information unavailable at decision time.;;Overfitting: Fits sample-specific variation.;;Multiple testing: Many trials raise false-discovery risk.|An analyst tests 100 unrelated strategies at a 5% false-positive threshold.~~Roughly five positives are expected on average under true nulls and calibrated tests.;;Record the full search history.;;Use appropriate correction and independent validation.~~One attractive result among many trials is weak evidence.~~Dependence among tests changes the full distribution.|Track all trials.;;Protect evaluation data.;;Demand economic rationale and robustness.
T38.11|A realistic backtest includes transaction costs, market impact, borrowing constraints and executable prices. Turnover and asset liquidity can erase a small gross signal. Capacity matters because a strategy that works with tiny trades may fail with large capital.~~Execution assumptions should reflect information timing and market access. Using a day's close after deciding with that close can embed an impossible fill.|Explicit costs: Fees and taxes.;;Impact/spread: Price concession from execution.;;Capacity constraint: Capital limited by market liquidity.|A strategy earns 12% gross with annual turnover 400% and assumed cost 0.5% per traded amount.~~Approximate trading drag is 2%.;;Net return is about 10% before other costs.;;Stress larger orders and short-borrow charges.~~Small unit costs accumulate with turnover.~~Turnover definitions and exact compounding must be consistent.|Use executable price timing.;;Model all costs.;;Test capacity and financing constraints.
T38.12|Monte Carlo simulation generates many outcomes from a specified model to analyse distributions, valuation or risk. Inputs include random processes, dependence and behavioural rules. More paths improve numerical precision but do not make the economic assumptions correct.~~Report uncertainty from both sampling and model choice. Rare events may require specialised methods to estimate reliably.|Process specification: Defines how variables evolve.;;Scenario generation: Samples paths from that process.;;Aggregation: Summarises outcomes and uncertainty.|A model simulates 10,000 project outcomes, with 800 negative NPVs.~~Estimated failure frequency is 8% under the model.;;Check assumptions about demand and costs.;;Repeat with alternative dependence and tail behaviour.~~The result is conditional on the model.~~It is not a guaranteed real-world probability.|Document distributions and dependence.;;Check convergence and reproducibility.;;Stress model alternatives and tails.
T38.13|Numerical pricing approximates values when closed-form solutions are unavailable or impractical. Optimisation selects a solution under an objective and constraints. Solver success means a mathematical criterion was met, not that the inputs, constraints or economics are appropriate.~~Check stability, bounds and sensitivity. An unconstrained optimum may recommend impossible leverage or short positions.|Numerical integration/lattice: Approximates valuation.;;Optimisation: Selects weights or decisions under rules.;;Sensitivity analysis: Tests dependence on inputs.|An optimiser allocates 150% to one asset and −50% to another, maximising a model objective.~~Recognise embedded leverage and shorting.;;Check whether those positions are permitted.;;Rerun with realistic liquidity and exposure constraints.~~The output can be mathematically valid but operationally infeasible.~~Estimated expected returns can make weights unstable.|Define feasible constraints.;;Check numerical stability.;;Test sensitivity to uncertain inputs.
T38.14|Algorithmic execution automates order placement toward an execution objective; systematic investing applies explicit rules to portfolio decisions. The two are related but distinct. Algorithms can create feedback, operational and control risks even when the investment thesis is sound.~~Include kill switches, monitoring and exception handling. Automation should be tested against realistic market disruptions rather than only normal conditions.|Execution algorithm: Controls trade timing and participation.;;Systematic strategy: Determines investment positions by rules.;;Operational control: Limits and stops unintended actions.|An execution rule buys 10% of observed market volume, but a data error multiplies volume by ten.~~The intended participation becomes 100% of true volume.;;Apply independent quantity and value limits.;;Halt and investigate abnormal input changes.~~A simple data defect can become a large trading error.~~Actual controls depend on venue and strategy.|Separate investment and execution logic.;;Set independent limits.;;Test outages, bad data and emergency stops.
T38.15|Machine learning can detect patterns for credit, fraud and forecasting, but depends on labelled data, stable relationships and suitable evaluation. High overall accuracy can hide poor detection of rare harmful events. Explainability, fairness and lawful data use matter in financial decisions.~~Performance should be monitored after deployment as populations change. A predictive score is not a causal explanation or automatic authority to decide.|Classification: Predicts categories or event probabilities.;;Regression: Predicts numerical outcomes.;;Drift monitoring: Tracks changing data and performance.|Only 1% of 10,000 transactions are fraudulent; a model labels all legitimate.~~Accuracy is 99%.;;Fraud recall is 0%.;;Evaluate precision, recall and costs at useful thresholds.~~High accuracy can describe a useless fraud detector.~~Ground-truth quality and delayed labels affect evaluation.|Use task-appropriate metrics.;;Validate fairness and data rights.;;Monitor drift and human escalation.
T38.16|Natural-language processing extracts or classifies information from disclosures and other text. Financial meaning depends on context, tables, negation and document scope. A keyword count can mistake risk disclosure for actual exposure or mix boilerplate with new information.~~Link extracted claims back to the original document and date. Models need evaluation against representative, manually reviewed examples.|Extraction: Identifies facts and entities.;;Classification: Assigns text categories.;;Semantic analysis: Examines meaning and relationships.|A report says the company has no exposure to a named market, but a keyword rule flags the market name.~~Inspect the surrounding sentence and negation.;;Verify against relevant tables.;;Correct the extraction and evaluate similar cases.~~Mention is not equivalent to exposure.~~Document formatting and scope can introduce further errors.|Retain source passages and dates.;;Test negation and tables.;;Use human review for material conclusions.
T38.17|AI can assist research, summarisation and modelling, but fluent output is not evidence of accuracy. Financial work requires source verification, calculation checks and transparent assumptions. Confidentiality, data rights and human accountability remain important.~~A model-generated citation can be nonexistent or support a different claim. Verify primary evidence and keep an audit trail before using outputs in decisions.|Research assistance: Finds and summarises candidate evidence.;;Model assistance: Helps generate or inspect calculations.;;Decision support: Organises alternatives under human review.|An AI summary reports revenue ₹120 lakh while the annual report states ₹102.~~Open the original statement.;;Check period, units and consolidation.;;Correct downstream calculations and record the source.~~One factual error can propagate through an attractive valuation.~~Tool output must be validated at material points.|Verify sources and arithmetic.;;Protect confidential inputs.;;Record assumptions, edits and accountable review.
T38.18|Reproducibility means another authorised analyst can regenerate results from documented data, code and environment. Model governance assigns ownership, approval, monitoring and change control. These practices make errors discoverable and results reviewable; they do not establish that a model is economically correct.~~Preserve input versions and decision dates. A rerun on revised data may differ legitimately from the original analysis.|Reproducible run: Recorded inputs and steps.;;Version control: Tracks changes in code and assumptions.;;Governance: Assigns review, approval and monitoring.|Two analysts run the same script but obtain different valuations because one uses a revised earnings dataset.~~Compare input versions and timestamps.;;Record the intended information date.;;Reconcile code, parameters and dependencies.~~Same code is insufficient without controlled inputs.~~Access and licensing can limit what may be shared.|Version data and assumptions.;;Keep an audit trail.;;Assign review and change responsibilities.
